# ULX design system consumption (application teams)

Guidance for **application** and **demo** code that **uses** ULX/ULS—not for implementing the ULX component library source itself (follow your repository’s internal standards for that).

## Prefer the system first

- **Components**: Use ULX components and their public arguments (variants, sizes, labels, slots) instead of rebuilding the same UI with raw elements and custom CSS.
- **Tokens and utilities**: Prefer existing utilities from `utill.less` (spacing, flex, typography, color utilities, etc.) and shared ULX/ULS variables and themes before adding new styling.
- **Custom CSS and inline styles**: Do **not** add new stylesheets, ad-hoc classes, or `style="..."` attributes when the design system already provides the same layout, color, or spacing. Custom styling is only appropriate when **no** existing component API, utility, or documented override can express the requirement.

## Overrides and extension

- Put intentional brand or app-specific overrides in the **approved** override/theme locations (for example ULX editor overrides and foundation overrides under `styles/editor`, `styles/gulx`, and `styles/foundation`, or your app’s documented override entry points)—not scattered in feature templates.
- Extend **tokens** or **utilities** in the proper LESS layers when something is missing; do not invent new utility class names in templates—add them only through the design system’s approved LESS entry points.

## Stable integration

- **Avoid brittle selectors** on internal `.ulx-*` markup or deeply nested structure that components do not document. Prefer public component APIs, wrapper layout in app code, or documented extension points. Undocumented selectors can break on upgrades.

## Agent-oriented workflow

- For building or styling **app** screens with ULX, use the **`ulx-consumer-ui`** skill and the pattern skills (`ulx-form-patterns`, `ulx-ui-patterns`, `ulx-data-display-patterns`, `ulx-component-discovery`) as needed.
